---
/**
 * The presentation card — the Presentations index, and the Presentation branch's only
 * card.
 *
 * ── THE BOX IS THE SIGNAL, AND IT IS DECIDED BY CONTENT ──────────────────────
 *
 * A presentation WITH a poster is boxed — surface, hairline border, `radius-2`. One
 * without sits directly on the page ground with no boundary. DESIGN.md's Elevation
 * section states the principle: boxed cards read as richer and more worth clicking,
 * which is exactly the claim a poster makes.
 *
 * THAT IS A THIRD, DISTINCT BOX RULE, which is why this is its own component rather
 * than an ArticleCard variant. The three differ in KIND, not in degree:
 *
 *   ArticleCard        boxed always                    unconditional
 *   NoteCard           boxed below md, bare from md    viewport-conditional
 *   PresentationCard   boxed iff it has a poster       content-conditional
 *
 * A shared component expressing all three would need a `variant` prop, which is the
 * thing ArticleCard's own header argues against — "makes every consumer restate a
 * breakpoint the card can measure for itself, and can be wired up wrong."
 *
 * ── THE BORDER IS DECLARED TRANSPARENT, NOT ABSENT ───────────────────────────
 *
 * NoteCard's reasoning, and it applies here for a second reason. A border appearing
 * from nothing on hover would shift the contents 1px — but more importantly, THE BOARD
 * DRAWS BOTH VARIANTS WITH THE SAME 16px PADDING (verified: the boxed card's inner
 * frame sits at x=16,y=16 and so does the unboxed one's). So an unboxed card is the
 * same box with its border and fill turned off, and its TEXT therefore lands on the
 * same vertical line as a boxed neighbour's. DESIGN.md: "on an index an unboxed card's
 * hover should match the boxed cards beside it, not exceed them."
 *
 * `:has(.card-media)` rather than a `boxed` prop, mirroring ArticleCard's use of the
 * same selector: the poster either rendered or it did not, so the box cannot fall out
 * of step with what is on screen the way a separately-passed boolean could.
 *
 * ── GEOMETRY, READ OFF THE BOARD (767:1922 boxed, 875:4218 bare) ─────────────
 *
 *   boxed  316x362  pad 16, title h58(2ln), 8, media 284x160, 16, eyebrow h24,
 *                   8, venue h27, 8, tags h21
 *   bare   316x128  pad 16, title h29(1ln), 8, eyebrow h24, 8, venue h27
 *
 * So the gap is `card-gap` (8) EVERYWHERE except after the media, which is 16. That is
 * the identical relationship ArticleCard encodes, and it is expressed the same way —
 * as the subtraction, so it survives either token moving. 284 x 9/16 = 159.75, the
 * board's 160, confirming the media is 16/9 as on the article card.
 *
 * ── NO CONTAINER QUERY, DELIBERATELY ─────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * ArticleCard carries one because it is genuinely rendered at two widths — 571 on the
 * home page and 316 in the masonry. This card has ONE consumer at ONE width, so a
 * horizontal form would be a layout nothing asks for, and the `.card-inner` wrapper
 * that a container query forces would exist to serve it. Flat until a second width
 * exists; ArticleCard's header records what to do when one does.
 */
import SanityImage from '../SanityImage.astro'
import Eyebrow from '../Eyebrow.astro'
import VisuallyHidden from '../VisuallyHidden.astro'
import type {SanityImageSource} from '../../sanity/image'

/**
 * A derived marker. `label` is what shows; `hint` is the expansion for assistive
 * technology, and only `+N` needs one — see the note on `.visually-hidden` below.
 */
export interface CardTag {
  label: string
  hint?: string
}

interface Props {
  href: string
  title: string
  genre?: string | null
  date?: string | null
  /** Already composed by `formatLocation` — "Seattle, WA", "Online". */
  venue?: string | null
  poster?: SanityImageSource | null
  tags?: CardTag[]
  /** Heading level, so a card can sit under whatever precedes it. */
  as?: 'h2' | 'h3' | 'h4'
  class?: string
  /* Forwarded to the root — the index's `data-genre` / `data-topics`, and invisibly
     the parent's scope attribute, which is how a page styles this at all. */
  [key: string]: unknown
}

const {
  href,
  title,
  genre,
  date,
  venue,
  poster,
  tags = [],
  as: Tag = 'h3',
  class: className,
  ...rest
}: Props = Astro.props
---

<article class:list={['preso-card', className]} {...rest}>
  {
    /* The anchor is on the title; the whole card is the target, via a stretched
       pseudo-element. Keeps the accessible name to the title rather than announcing
       title, eyebrow, venue and every tag as one link. See ArticleCard for the full
       note — including the cost, which is that a second link in here would need
       lifting with `z-index`. This card has none. */
  }
  <Tag class="card-title"><a href={href}>{title}</a></Tag>

  {
    poster?.asset && (
      <div class="card-media">
        {/* Decorative: the title sits directly above it. Same call ArticleCard makes,
            and it is why no `altText` is projected for the card's poster. */}
        <SanityImage image={poster} aspect="16/9" alt="" />
      </div>
    )
  }

  <div class="card-body">
    <Eyebrow genre={genre} date={date} />
    {venue && <p class="card-venue">{venue}</p>}

    {
      /*
      ── A LIST, AND `role="list"` IS NOT BELT-AND-BRACES ───────────────────
      These are discrete markers, so a list is the honest element — but
      `list-style: none` drops list semantics in Safari, which is why the role is
      restated. The facet chip row in the Insights index carries the same pair for
      the same reason.

      NOT LINKS AND NOT BUTTONS. Every one of these is DERIVED — the query knows
      whether a transcript exists, not an editor — and they are informative rather
      than filterable. DESIGN.md's `tag` role is explicitly "a marker, deliberately
      not a control. No hover, pressed or focus", which is why this does not reach
      for <Chip>: that component is the `filter-chip` role and brings `:hover` and
      `:active` states this must not have.
    */
    }
    {
      tags.length > 0 && (
        <ul class="card-tags" role="list">
          {tags.map((tag) => (
            <li class="tag">
              {tag.label}
              {/*
                ── ONLY `+N` CARRIES ONE, AND THAT IS THE RULE ──────────────
                "transcript" and "audio" say what they are. "+2" does not — it means
                "delivered at two further venues", which is unguessable from two
                characters whether you can see it or not. The visible form stays terse
                because the board draws it terse and it sits directly beside the venue
                it counts from; the expansion makes it survive being read aloud out of
                that context.
              */}
              {tag.hint && <VisuallyHidden>{tag.hint}</VisuallyHidden>}
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      )
    }
  </div>
</article>

<style>
  /* ── The box ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .preso-card {
    display: grid;
    row-gap: var(--card-gap);

    /* `start`, not the default. A grid container's `align-content` is `normal`, which
       behaves as `stretch`, so a card shorter than its row grew its GAPS rather than
       leaving the slack at the bottom. Both other cards carry this and record the
       same finding. */
    align-content: start;

    /* Containing block for the full-card link overlay. */
    position: relative;

    /* Padding is on BOTH variants — see the header. The border is declared and
       transparent so promoting it changes a colour rather than adding a property. */
    padding: var(--card-pad);
    border: var(--border-width-hairline) solid transparent;
    border-radius: var(--radius-2);
    background-color: transparent;

    /* Eased at the 0.15s affordance end of DESIGN.md's convention, on the resting
       rule so it eases both in and out. */
    transition:
      border-color 0.15s,
      background-color 0.15s,
      box-shadow 0.15s;
  }

  /* THE POSTER PROMOTES THE BOX. Nothing else does. */
  .preso-card:has(.card-media) {
    border-color: var(--color-border);
    background-color: var(--color-surface);
  }

  /*
   * Hover resolves BOTH variants to the same treatment, which is the point: a bare
   * card gains the box, a boxed one already has it, and both take the accent
   * highlight. So the hover affordance is uniform across a row even though the
   * resting state deliberately is not. NoteCard does exactly this below `md`.
   *
   * The highlight is the hard 2px accent offset with no blur and no spread — see
   * DESIGN.md → Elevation & Depth for why that is not a breach of "no soft shadows".
   * No spread means it inherits `radius-2` and tapers at the corners.
   */
  .preso-card:hover,
  .preso-card:focus-within {
    border-color: var(--color-border);
    background-color: var(--color-surface);
    box-shadow: 2px 2px 0 0 var(--color-accent);
  }

  /* ── Type ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  /*
   * The card owns its type; `as` owns only the outline level. Without this, the index
   * passing `as="h2"` would draw the title at h2's size and tracking, which is not
   * what the board draws (58px for two lines = 29.25 each, which is `font-size-3` at
   * 1.3).
   *
   * THIS IS THE THIRD HOME FOR THE h3 ROLE — ArticleCard and NoteCard are the others,
   * and the article page's rail restates it too. ArticleCard's header nominates the
   * set; this adds to it rather than changing it. Promoting the shared card chrome is
   * queued as its own change so it does not ride along with a new page.
   */
  .card-title {
    font-size: var(--font-size-3);
    line-height: 1.3;
    letter-spacing: normal;
  }

  /* `text-title` and no underline — DESIGN.md: a title in its own block at heading
     size is identified as a link by position. */
  .card-title a {
    color: var(--color-text-title);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .card-title a:hover {
    color: var(--color-link-hover);
  }

  .card-title a::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
  }

  /* ── Media ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .card-media {
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
    overflow: hidden;
    border-radius: var(--radius-1);
  }

  /* base.css sets `height: auto` on every image, which is right for images that size
     themselves and wrong here, where the box is fixed by the wrapper's ratio. */
  .card-media img {
    inline-size: 100%;
    block-size: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
  }

  /* The row gap already supplies `card-gap`; this tops it up to the board's 16 below
     the media. Written as the subtraction so the relationship survives either token
     moving — ArticleCard encodes the identical rule. */
  .preso-card:has(.card-media) .card-body {
    margin-block-start: calc(var(--card-media-block) - var(--card-gap));
  }

  /* ── Body ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  /*
   * The body's own children sit at `card-gap`, matching the board's 8 between the
   * eyebrow, the venue and the tag row. A nested grid rather than margins so the
   * three are spaced by one declaration.
   */
  .card-body {
    display: grid;
    row-gap: var(--card-gap);
    align-content: start;
  }

  /*
   * `body-compact` — the same size step as body at 1.5 rather than 1.8, which is the
   * role's stated purpose ("a column too narrow for `body`"). The board draws this
   * line 27 tall, which is `font-size-2` at 1.5 exactly, and in the PROSE face rather
   * than Lato: it is content about the delivery, not apparatus about the card, so it
   * reads at body colour rather than `text-muted`. That is the visible difference from
   * the eyebrow directly above it.
   */
  .card-venue {
    font-size: var(--font-size-2);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  /* ── Tags ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .card-tags {
    /* base.css keeps the UA list indent for prose; a marker row is not prose. */
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;

    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    /* The board sets three tags 8 apart in both axes. */
    gap: var(--space-1);
  }

  /*
   * DESIGN.md's `tag` role: `surface-muted` fill, `text-muted`, Lato 700 at the 14px
   * step, `radius-1`, and NO BORDER — a tag is a marker, not a control, so it takes
   * neither the card hairline nor the chip's control border.
   *
   * ── THE PADDING IS INLINE-ONLY, AND THAT IS MEASURED ───────────────────────
   *
   * DESIGN.md gives `padding: space-1` as a single value, which read as all four sides
   * would make the tag 21 + 8 + 8 = 37 tall. THE BOARD DRAWS IT 21, which is the 14px
   * type at 1.5 — the line box exactly, so the block padding is zero.
   *
   * This is the same gap the `filter-chip` had: DESIGN.md carried its fill, border,
   * radius and typography but no padding or height, and `3px 12px` was derived by
   * measuring the board and then recorded back into DESIGN.md. This wants the same
   * treatment — worth adding to DESIGN.md → Filter chips as a sibling entry rather
   * than leaving the next consumer to re-measure 767:1922.
   *
   * Widths corroborate: at 8px inline padding "audio" computes to 53 against the
   * board's 52, and "+1" to 31 against 33.
   */
  .tag {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: baseline;

    padding: 0 var(--space-1);
    border-radius: var(--radius-1);
    background-color: var(--color-surface-muted);

    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    font-family: var(--font-heading);
    font-size: var(--font-size-0);
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  /* The `+N` tag's expansion is <VisuallyHidden>, which owns those declarations and
     the reasoning for each of them. Nothing about hiding it lives here. */
</style>
